1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,288
It is Tuesday, ten past one, and Aziz orders a kettle from the marketplace, on his work laptop.

2
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:11,736
That morning he paid his phone bill in the mobile bank, and checked a parcel in the marketplace app on his phone.

3
00:00:11,786 --> 00:00:14,976
Three doors into one bank, on two devices.

4
00:00:15,026 --> 00:00:17,376
So how many identities does Aziz have?

5
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:22,248
In Lesson 1, the map showed the apps, the gateway, Keycloak and the stores.

6
00:00:22,298 --> 00:00:29,688
This lesson looks inside one box on that map: the customer, and how one person stays one person in every app.

7
00:00:29,738 --> 00:00:32,592
Start with the model that most teams build first.

8
00:00:32,642 --> 00:00:36,264
Each app keeps its own customers, with their own passwords.

9
00:00:36,314 --> 00:00:38,256
The mobile bank has an Aziz.

10
00:00:38,306 --> 00:00:40,464
The marketplace has an Aziz.

11
00:00:40,514 --> 00:00:42,720
The credit service has an Aziz.

12
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:45,768
Each one has a password, and each one has a level.

13
00:00:45,818 --> 00:00:50,928
Aziz passes MyID in the mobile bank, and that Aziz is at level two.

14
00:00:50,978 --> 00:00:56,352
But the marketplace Aziz is still at level one, so the marketplace asks for proof again.

15
00:00:56,402 --> 00:01:00,672
Now a bank operator sees a strange login in the marketplace.

16
00:01:00,722 --> 00:01:04,440
She wants to end his marketplace sessions, and only those.

17
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:10,488
In this model she cannot: the sessions do not know their app, and the three Azizes do not know each other.

18
00:01:10,538 --> 00:01:17,880
I think this is where most identity bugs start: one person, three passwords to steal, and three levels that disagree.

19
00:01:17,930 --> 00:01:19,920
heeyID turns the picture around.

20
00:01:19,970 --> 00:01:22,440
At the centre is one person.

21
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:28,584
The person record keeps the personal identification number, the level and the verified identity.

22
00:01:28,634 --> 00:01:30,456
Under it is one phone number.

23
00:01:30,506 --> 00:01:33,072
The phone number is the login for every app.

24
00:01:33,122 --> 00:01:35,280
Next to it is one password.

25
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:40,824
The apps have no passwords of their own, so a password change applies in every app at once.

26
00:01:40,874 --> 00:01:45,288
The level belongs to the person, so it is the same in every app.

27
00:01:45,338 --> 00:01:47,160
Now the apps.

28
00:01:47,210 --> 00:01:50,448
Each app keeps one account of the person: an app account.

29
00:01:50,498 --> 00:01:55,272
The platform makes it the first time Aziz registers or logs in to that app.

30
00:01:55,322 --> 00:02:00,024
Aziz has three: the mobile bank, the marketplace and the credit service.

31
00:02:00,074 --> 00:02:04,200
Each app account has its sessions, one for each phone or browser.

32
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:08,064
In the mobile bank, Aziz has one session, on his phone.

33
00:02:08,114 --> 00:02:13,152
In the marketplace, he has two: one on his phone, and one in the browser on his laptop.

34
00:02:13,202 --> 00:02:16,128
Each session records its app and its device.

35
00:02:16,178 --> 00:02:21,600
Its tokens are for its own app only, so a marketplace token never opens the mobile bank.

36
00:02:21,650 --> 00:02:24,840
Lesson 5 shows how Keycloak does this.

37
00:02:24,890 --> 00:02:26,712
Back to the operator.

38
00:02:26,762 --> 00:02:28,800
Now she has three choices.

39
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:31,512
She can end one session: the laptop.

40
00:02:31,562 --> 00:02:35,712
She can end all the sessions of one app: the whole marketplace.

41
00:02:35,762 --> 00:02:38,736
Or she can end all the sessions of the person.

42
00:02:38,786 --> 00:02:42,312
Every app keeps working, and the level does not move.

43
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:46,776
One more thing sits next to the phone number: a log of every check.

44
00:02:46,826 --> 00:02:52,032
A code from the bank's SMS service, in the marketplace, at nine fifteen.

45
00:02:52,082 --> 00:02:55,944
A TAF check, in the mobile bank, at nine twenty.

46
00:02:55,994 --> 00:02:59,040
A MyID check, in the mobile bank, last week.

47
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:01,968
Each line has the provider, the app and the time.

48
00:03:02,018 --> 00:03:06,024
So the bank always knows how it knows that this number is Aziz's.

49
00:03:06,074 --> 00:03:08,712
But not every app may do every check.

50
00:03:08,762 --> 00:03:10,920
The mobile bank is the master app.

51
00:03:10,970 --> 00:03:17,448
It moves money, so only the master app runs MyID, TAF and the app self-protection.

52
00:03:17,498 --> 00:03:24,984
Every other app is a side app: the marketplace app, the marketplace web profile, and the ordering, delivery and service apps.

53
00:03:25,034 --> 00:03:27,048
A side app never checks a face.

54
00:03:27,098 --> 00:03:30,696
In a side app, Aziz types his phone number and his password.

55
00:03:30,746 --> 00:03:37,248
Then he approves the login in the master app, or he types an SMS code from the bank's SMS service.

56
00:03:37,298 --> 00:03:41,424
A customer can even use the side apps with no account in the master app at all.

57
00:03:41,474 --> 00:03:45,672
Now draw a frame around everything you see: one installation.

58
00:03:45,722 --> 00:03:52,104
One installation serves one bank, with one Keycloak realm, and each app is one client in it.

59
00:03:52,154 --> 00:03:54,552
There is no tenant column anywhere.

60
00:03:54,602 --> 00:03:59,976
A second bank gets its own installation, so no request can ever read across two banks.

61
00:04:00,026 --> 00:04:04,296
To recap: one person, one phone number, one password.

62
00:04:04,346 --> 00:04:08,592
One account in each app, and one session on each device.

63
00:04:08,642 --> 00:04:11,184
Only the master app proves who he is.

64
00:04:11,234 --> 00:04:13,584
So how many identities does Aziz have?

65
00:04:13,634 --> 00:04:14,400
One.

66
00:04:14,450 --> 00:04:16,272
But look at the laptop again.

67
00:04:16,322 --> 00:04:23,808
Aziz is at level two, yet his new laptop session starts at level one, until he approves it in the mobile bank.

68
00:04:23,858 --> 00:04:25,584
So a session has a level too.

69
00:04:25,634 --> 00:04:27,456
What does level really mean?

70
00:04:27,506 --> 00:04:29,760
Next: the words of identity.
